      "value": "# Examples Showcase\nBefore/after prompt examples demonstrating the impact of good prompt engineering.\n\n---\n\n## How to Use This Showcase\n\nThese examples demonstrate the **CTF Framework** (Context, Task, Format) and **RTCCO Framework** (Role, Task, Context, Constraints, Output) in action. Use them as:\n- Learning examples for understanding prompt structure\n- Templates you can adapt for your own needs\n- Proof of why prompt engineering matters\n\n---\n\n## Example 1: RFI Analysis\n\n### ❌ BEFORE (Weak Prompt)\n```\nHelp me with this RFI:\n\nThe electrical subcontractor is asking about conduit routing. \nThey say there's a conflict with the HVAC. What should I do?\n```\n\n**Problems:**\n- No context about project type, size, phase\n- No specific question or task defined\n- No requested output format\n- AI has to guess what \"help\" means\n\n### ✅ AFTER (Strong Prompt — CTF Framework)\n```\nCONTEXT:\nI'm a project manager on a $15M commercial TI project in downtown San Diego. \nWe're in the construction phase, 60% complete. The electrical subcontractor \nsubmitted an RFI stating that the conduit routing shown on drawings E-201 \nthrough E-205 conflicts with HVAC ductwork in the ceiling plenum. The conflict \naffects approximately 2,000 SF of office space on the 3rd floor. We're on a \ntight schedule with tenant move-in in 90 days.\n\nTASK:\nAnalyze this RFI and provide:\n1. A root cause assessment of why this conflict wasn't caught in coordination\n2. Three potential solutions with pros/cons for each\n3. A draft RFI response to send to the architect\n4. Recommendations for preventing similar issues going forward\n\nFORMAT:\nProvide your response in a structured format with:\n- Executive Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Root Cause Analysis\n- Solutions Matrix (table format with Solution, Pros, Cons, Cost Impact, Schedule Impact)\n- Draft RFI Response (ready to copy/paste)\n- Prevention Recommendations (bullet points)\n```\n\n**Why It Works:**\n- **Context**: Project size, type, phase, schedule pressure\n- **Task**: Four specific deliverables requested\n- **Format**: Clear structure for output\n- AI knows exactly what to produce\n\n**Sample Output Improvement:**\n| Aspect | Weak Prompt Result | Strong Prompt Result |\n|--------|-------------------|---------------------|\n| Specificity | Generic advice | Tailored to $15M TI project |\n| Solutions | 1-2 vague ideas | 3 detailed options with impacts |\n| Usability | Needs significant editing | Ready to use |\n| Time saved | None (many follow-ups needed) | Significant (one-shot response) |\n\n---\n\n## Example 2: Change Order Review\n\n### ❌ BEFORE (Weak Prompt)\n```\nThis change order seems expensive. Is $85,000 fair for adding \n10 more electrical outlets?\n```\n\n**Problems:**\n- No breakdown of the CO provided\n- No project context\n- \"Fair\" is undefined — by what standard?\n- No output format specified\n\n### ✅ AFTER (Strong Prompt — RTCCO Framework)\n```\nROLE:\nAct as an experienced construction cost estimator and contract administrator.\n\nTASK:\nEvaluate this change order for fairness, accuracy, and contract compliance.\n\nCONTEXT:\n- Project: $8M office renovation, lump sum contract\n- Phase: Construction, 40% complete\n- Contract type: AIA A133, GMP with shared savings\n- Change Order #7: Add 10 duplex receptacles in executive offices\n- CO amount: $85,000\n- Justification: Owner-requested addition per meeting on 3/15\n\nBreakdown provided by EC:\n- Material: $12,000 (1200' MC cable, 10 devices, plates, etc.)\n- Labor: $48,000 (480 hours @ $100/hr)\n- Overhead & Profit: $15,000\n- Sales tax: $10,000\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Reference RSMeans or similar for fair pricing\n- Consider productivity factors for working in occupied space\n- Check for potential unbalanced pricing\n- Note any contract clause implications\n\nOUTPUT:\nProvide:\n1. **Pricing Analysis** — Line-by-line comparison to fair market rates\n2. **Red Flags** — Any concerns about the breakdown\n3. **Fair Price Recommendation** — What you would consider reasonable\n4. **Negotiation Strategy** — Key points to discuss with the EC\n5. **Contract Notes** — Any clause considerations for approval\n```\n\n**Why It Works:**\n- **Role**: Sets AI as cost estimator perspective\n- **Context**: Contract type, phase, full breakdown\n- **Constraints**: Reference standards, specific checks\n- **Output**: Five-part structured deliverable\n\n---\n\n## Example 3: Submittal Review\n\n### ❌ BEFORE (Weak Prompt)\n```\nReview this submittal for the carpet. It's spec section 09 68 00.\n```\n\n**Problems:**\n- No attachment or content from the submittal\n- No spec requirements stated\n- No output format\n- \"Review\" is ambiguous — for what?\n\n### ✅ AFTER (Strong Prompt)\n```\nCONTEXT:\nI'm reviewing a carpet submittal for a Class A office building lobby renovation.\n- Spec section: 09 68 00 Carpet\n- Submittal includes: Product data, samples, installation instructions\n- Key spec requirements from 09 68 00:\n  - Type: Solution-dyed nylon, 28 oz minimum\n  - Pattern: As selected by Architect from manufacturer's full range\n  - Fire rating: Class I per NFPA 253\n  - Warranty: Minimum 10-year commercial use\n  - CRI Green Label Plus certified\n\nTASK:\nReview the attached submittal for compliance with these requirements.\nNote any deviations, missing information, or coordination items.\n\nFORMAT:\nProvide a formal submittal review in this format:\n1. **Submittal Information** (Submittal #, Date, Subcontractor, Product)\n2. **Compliance Status**: [ ] Approved / [ ] Approved as Noted / [ ] Revise and Resubmit / [ ] Rejected\n3. **Compliance Checklist** (table with Requirement | Submitted | Compliant? | Notes)\n4. **Deviations from Specifications**\n5. **Missing Information**\n6. **Coordination Items** (with other trades/spec sections)\n7. **Recommended Response Letter**\n```\n\n**Why It Works:**\n- Includes actual spec requirements\n- Clear compliance categories\n- Provides checklist format\n- Requests actionable output (response letter)\n\n---\n\n## Example 4: Safety Meeting Minutes\n\n### ❌ BEFORE (Weak Prompt)\n```\nWrite up notes from our safety meeting. We talked about fall protection \nand the new hard hat policy.\n```\n\n### ✅ AFTER (Strong Prompt)\n```\nCONTEXT:\n- Meeting: Weekly jobsite safety toolbox talk\n- Project: 12-story mixed-use building, $45M, structural phase\n- Date: March 25, 2026\n- Attendees: 45 workers (GC and major subcontractors)\n- Superintendent leading: John Martinez\n\nTopics discussed:\n1. Fall protection reminder after near-miss on 8th floor\n2. New hard hat policy: Type I helmets required starting April 1\n3. Heat illness prevention (upcoming summer)\n4. Q&A about traffic control at the loading dock\n\nTASK:\nGenerate professional safety meeting minutes suitable for the project file \nand potential OSHA documentation.\n\nFORMAT:\nUse this structure:\n1. **Header** (Project name, meeting type, date, location, attendance count)\n2. **Topics Covered** (with key points from each)\n3. **Incidents/Near-Misses Discussed**\n4. **Action Items** (table with Item, Responsible Party, Due Date)\n5. **Questions Raised and Answers**\n6. **Next Meeting Date**\n7. **Signatures block** (placeholder for Superintendent and Safety Officer)\n\nTone: Professional, suitable for official project records.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Example 5: Email to Owner\n\n### ❌ BEFORE (Weak Prompt)\n```\nWrite an email to the owner about the delay. The concrete pour \ngot pushed back because of rain.\n```\n\n### ✅ AFTER (Strong Prompt)\n```\nCONTEXT:\n- Project: $22M office building, GMP contract\n- Owner contact: Sarah Chen, ABC Properties\n- GC Project Manager: I'm writing this email\n- Situation: Scheduled foundation pour for March 26 was delayed due to \n  2.5\" of unexpected rainfall. New pour date: March 29.\n- Schedule impact: 3 calendar days, but within float; no impact to \n  Substantial Completion date\n- Contract: Weather delays are excusable but not compensable (GC bears risk)\n\nTASK:\nDraft a professional email to the owner notifying them of the delay, \nexplaining the situation, and confirming no impact to the overall schedule.\n\nFORMAT:\nStandard business email with:\n- Professional but approachable tone\n- Clear subject line\n- Brief explanation without being defensive\n- Confirmation of no impact to completion date\n- Offer to discuss if questions\n\nTONE: Professional, confident, not apologetic (weather is beyond our control).\n```\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways from Examples\n\n### The Pattern of Good Prompts:\n\n1. **Set the stage** — Project type, size, phase, stakeholders\n2. **Define the task** — Specific, numbered deliverables\n3. **Provide constraints** — What to reference, what to avoid\n4. **Request a format** — Structure you can actually use\n5. **Set the tone** — Professional, casual, direct, diplomatic\n\n### Common Mistakes:\n\n| Mistake | Why It Hurts | Fix |\n|---------|--------------|-----|\n| No context | AI guesses project type/size | Include relevant background |\n| Vague task | AI doesn't know what to produce | Number your deliverables |\n| No format | AI dumps unstructured text | Specify sections/tables |\n| Missing constraints | AI may not check key items | Tell it what to verify |\n| No tone guidance | May be too formal or too casual | Specify audience relationship |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Prompt Improvement Checklist\n\nBefore submitting a prompt, ask yourself:\n\n- [ ] **Context**: Did I include project type, size, phase, relevant background?\n- [ ] **Task**: Did I clearly state what I want, in specific terms?\n- [ ] **Format**: Did I request a specific output structure?\n- [ ] **Constraints**: Did I note any limitations, references, or requirements?\n- [ ] **Tone**: Did I specify the appropriate tone for the audience?\n\n---\n\n## Contribute Your Examples\n\nAs we work together, I'll add successful before/after examples to this showcase. If you have a prompt that worked particularly well (or a bad one you learned from), let me know and we'll document it!",
      "limit": 100000,
      "project_id": null,
      "template_name": null,
      "is_template": false,
